// tests/engine/all_headers.cpp.in — the template CMake fills with an #include of EVERY header under
// engine/include/maz. Configured into the build directory at configure time, so the list can never go
// stale: a header added to the engine is in this test the next time CMake runs, with nothing to remember.
//
// What it proves is something no per-module test can: that the engine's headers can be used TOGETHER.
// Maz is header-only and its 690-odd headers all live in a handful of namespaces, so two of them
// declaring the same name is not a style problem, it is a build failure for whoever includes both — and
// every existing test includes one module, which is exactly the arrangement that cannot see it. When
// this was first run it found five collisions that had been sitting in the engine unnoticed:
//
//   * maz::math::Frame           — ParallelTransport.hpp and RotationMinimizingFrame.hpp, one each,
//                                  so comparing the two frame builders was a compile error
//   * maz::math::simplifyPolyline— the same Ramer-Douglas-Peucker written out twice, in Geometry2D.hpp
//                                  and SimplifyPolyline.hpp
//   * maz::math::closestPointOnSegment — two different functions, three vec2 arguments each, with the
//                                  point and the segment in the OPPOSITE order
//   * maz::math::clampf          — MathFuncs.hpp's, and a second copy in Sdf2D.hpp reached through a
//                                  `using namespace detail2d`
//   * maz::render::lerpColor     — ColorOps.hpp's, and a differently-behaved one (clamping, alpha
//                                  ignored) in MeshVertexColorGradient.hpp
//
// If this stops compiling, that is the finding: two headers have collided. Fix the collision rather than
// dropping a header from the list — the list is generated and there is nowhere to drop it from.
//
// It is a compile-time check wearing a test's clothes: the executable runs, prints, and exits 0. The
// build either produced it or it did not.

@MAZ_ALL_HEADER_INCLUDES@

#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    std::printf("all_headers: OK — every header under engine/include/maz compiles in one file (%d of them).\n",
                @MAZ_ALL_HEADER_COUNT@);
    return 0;
}
